    {
        "content": "<p>Hi all,</p>\n<p>I've formalized a normalized polynomial-kernel form of the one-variable formal Lagrange inversion theorem, and I'd like some feedback before opening a PR. The current statement is:</p>\n<p>lean</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">PowerSeries.lagrange_inversion_coeff</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Field</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CharZero</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hφ0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">φ.coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"bp\">⟦</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"bp\">⟧</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hfix</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">PowerSeries.X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Polynomial.aeval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">PowerSeries.coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>In words: suppose <code>φ</code> is a polynomial with constant term 1 and <code>B</code> satisfies the fixed-point equation <code>B = X · φ(B)</code>. Then for <code>n ≥ 1</code>, the <code>n</code>-th coefficient of <code>B</code> equals <code>[X^{n-1}] φ^n / n</code>.</p>\n<p>The proof is purely algebraic and doesn't use analytic convergence. It works with residues of formal Laurent series. Everything is in one new file of about 505 lines, <code>Mathlib/RingTheory/PowerSeries/LagrangeInversion.lean</code>, and both <code>lake build Mathlib</code> and <code>lake exe lint-style</code> pass.</p>\n<p>Branch: <a href=\"https://github.com/manman4/mathlib4/tree/codex/power-series-lagrange-inversion\">codex/power-series-lagrange-inversion</a></p>\n<p>For transparency, the original Lean proof was written with the help of Codex. I have gone through the whole proof myself and understand the main theorem and all of the auxiliary lemmas.</p>\n<p>I'd especially appreciate opinions on the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Is this worth having in Mathlib?</strong> Lagrange inversion is a standard tool in enumerative combinatorics (e.g. counting trees), but I'd like to know whether it's a good fit.</li>\n<li><strong>File location.</strong> Is <code>Mathlib/RingTheory/PowerSeries/LagrangeInversion.lean</code> a reasonable place?</li>\n<li><strong>Generality of the kernel.</strong> Right now <code>φ : K[X]</code> with <code>φ.coeff 0 = 1</code>. Is that enough for a first version, or should it handle general formal power series?</li>\n<li><strong>Naming.</strong> Does <code>PowerSeries.lagrange_inversion_coeff</code> follow Mathlib naming conventions?</li>\n<li><strong>Proof strategy.</strong> Is the formal Laurent series residue argument an acceptable approach, or would reviewers prefer a different route?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Thanks in advance!</p>",
        "id": 624591675,
        "sender_full_name": "Seiichi Manyama",
        "timestamp": 1789551828
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For the project <a href=\"https://github.com/Julian-Kuelshammer/ExtensionClosedSubcategoriesOfAn/\">https://github.com/Julian-Kuelshammer/ExtensionClosedSubcategoriesOfAn/</a> I had Aristotle recently formalise a version of this as well. I didn't clean it up. Here is the Lean statements Aristotle produced:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Polynomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"bp\">⟦</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"bp\">⟧</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">coeff_pow_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hY</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">PowerSeries</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Polynomial</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">aeval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">,</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">PowerSeries</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and an H-form:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">coeff_aeval_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hY</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">PowerSeries</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Polynomial</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">aeval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Polynomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">PowerSeries</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Polynomial</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">aeval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Polynomial</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">derivative</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>When looking around for sources to write up a mathematical article which uses this result, I stumbled upon <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17576\">https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17576</a>, which gives a simple proof by induction as well as some discussion about what would be called \"Implementation details\" around here, that might be worth contemplating before a PR.</p>",
        "id": 624599574,
        "sender_full_name": "Julian Külshammer",
        "timestamp": 1789553879
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"317890\">@Julian Külshammer</span></p>\n<p>Thanks a lot for sharing these statements and the reference!</p>\n<p>Looking at them, I now see that <code>coeff_pow_eq</code> is the <code>H(w) = w^k</code> specialization of the Lagrange–Bürmann formula expressed by <code>coeff_aeval_eq</code>. The coefficient identity in my current theorem is then the further specialization <code>k = 1</code>, although my statement is over a general characteristic-zero field rather than only over <code>ℚ</code>.</p>\n<p>I'll look into the inductive proof from the paper and try to reorganize my development around the stronger statements, with <code>coeff_aeval_eq</code> as the main result and <code>coeff_pow_eq</code> as its specialization.</p>\n<p>Is the Lean code that Aristotle produced available somewhere I could look at? Also, since we've both worked on this, I'd be happy to coordinate so that we don't end up duplicating effort in getting a version into Mathlib.</p>",
        "id": 624604143,
        "sender_full_name": "Seiichi Manyama",
        "timestamp": 1789555134
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Julian shared an induction-based proof, which I used as the basis for the formalization.<br>\nSince it gives a more general statement with a shorter proof than my original version, I adopted that formulation.</p>",
